Porous and flexible membrane derived from ZIF-8-decorated hyphae for outstanding adsorption of $\mathbf{P6^{2+}}$ ion
Abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) based membranes with superior mechanical properties are of particular interest in purification, separation, and catalysis. Nevertheless, their fabrication still remains a grand challenge. Here, fungus hyphae (Mucor) were used as a robust scaffold to load the MOFs and induced the formation of porous and flexible membranes. ZIF-8 was used as a representative of MOFs. The ZIF8@Mucor membrane was formed by the in-situ growth of ZIF-8 on hyphae and then a vacuum filtration of the ZIF-8/hyphae composite. ZIF-8 was effectively dispersed on the ZIF-8@Mucor membrane, and the shear modulus of ZIF-8@Mucor-3 was 864 MPa by calculation. The ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor membrane exhibited promising properties for adsorption application to remove the highly toxic $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ . The adsorption capacity of this membrane was as high as $1443.29~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Results from dynamic adsorption indicated that the penetration concentration of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions was less than $5\%$ of the original level before 80 minutes whereas after 160 minutes, penetration concentration of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions was more than $90\%$ . This study would open a new way of how to synthesize composite MOFs/bacterial membranes for energy and environment purposes.
Keywords: Membrane; ZIF-8; Fungus; Adsorption; Lead ion.
## 1. Introduction
Nowadays, heavy metals are becoming one of the most severe environmental problems. Typically, natural aquatic systems that are exposed to leads ( $\mathrm{\bf{Pb}})$ suffer many treats to their biodiversity [1-3]. Unlike organic contaminants, Pb(II) is nonbiodegradable and tends to accumulate in living organisms. Once Pb (II) finds its way into the human body, it causes sustained harm, such as the central nervous system breakdown, because of its high toxicity and accumulative effect [4]. There are various methods applied to remove $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ from wastewater, such as membrane filtration [5], coagulation [6], adsorption [7], ion exchange [8], precipitation [9]. Adsorption proves to be one of the most potent and cost-saving techniques [10]. And many adsorbents for removing of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ from wastewater were investigated [10-12].
In the adsorbent family, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have emerged as potential candidates for removing heavy metal ions effectively [13-16]. MOFs are capable for the treatment of a broad range of pollutants due to the fact that they have a tunable texture properties [17-19]. MOFs have significant optical and mechanical properties originating from its small size when they are as nanomaterials [20, 21]. Yu et al. prepared various ZIFs to adsorb three typical dyes, neutral rhodamine, anionic methyl orange, and cationic methylene blue, which achieved an adsorption capacity of about $20\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [22]. Wang et al. used six kinds of MOFs to adsorb Pb (II) and Zn (II) and found that zinc-based MOFs showed high selectivity in the adsorption of Pb (II) [23]. Despite all these enumerable advantages, MOFs are still facing  myriad of challenges because of the small size of particles. Consequently, MOFs cannot be directly used in practical applications, resulting in different operations, waste of resources and secondary pollution [24]. To alleviate these problems, some researchers combined membrane materials with MOFs crystal. Zhang et al. combined 6FDA-DAM polyimide with a zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8) to fabricate mixed matrix membranes. The ZIF-8 crystal was prone to fall off due to only a physical bond between the ZIF-8 crystal and the membrane [25].
The study employed a bio-assembly method, an advanced micro-particle method for solving problems. Microorganisms as vectors of microparticles have received extensive attention due to their high reproduction and cheap resource [26, 27]. Filamentous fungus, such as Mucor, is a kind of unique microorganism, which possesses both microscale and macroscale features[26]. Besides, fungus hyphae have abundant functional groups on the cell wall, such as phosphonate, hydroxy, and amino group. However, as for MOFs, the high reactivity of metal ions and organic ligands generally promotes their intramolecular self-assembly via coordination effect. For instance, Marion Gualino et al successfully prepared ZIF- $8@\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ membrane using $Z_{\mathrm{n}}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and 2-methylimidazole solution through the modified alumina film [28]. Aguado et al. synthesized in-situ, a substituted imidazolate-based MOF (SIM-1) membrane [29]. Bux et al. prepared a continuous ZIF-8 layer by putting ZIF-8 seed alumina discs into solvothermal secondary growth solutions [30]. In these conventional assembly processes, the formation and aggregation of MOFs molecules predominantly tend to occur in bulk solution rather than around support material surface, which greatly wastes expensive raw materials and lower performance.
In this study, a special phased growth of MOFs (ZIF-8) on hyphae surface was successfully induced to produce layer-by-layer assembly, avoiding the self-aggregation of MOFs molecules in bulk solution. This approach not only achieved the macroconstruction of MOFS molecules but also realized the controllable load of MOFs nanoparticles on a one-dimensional scale. Simultaneously, a very high adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Pb^{2+}\left(>1000\mathrm{mg/g\right)}}$ in water was obtained, far beyond most previous reports. Adsorption behavior was carefully explored.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Synthesis of ZIF-8/Mucor
Mucor. Mucor was cultured on $200~\mathrm{mL}$ potato culture medium (PDA) in $300~\mathrm{mL}$ Erlenmeyer flasks, shaking at 160 rpm and $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for three days. Each liter of PDA contains $200\ \mathrm{g}$ potato, $0.5\mathrm{~g~MgSO_{4}}$ , $1.0\mathrm{~g~KH}_{2}\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ , and $20\mathrm{~g~}$ glucose. PDA was autoclaved at $115~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 15 minutes before inoculation which was processed on an aseptic bench. The cultured Mucor was then filtered (G-2 sand core funnel filter) and rinsed with ultrapure water. The obtained Mucor was dispersed into ultrapure water to form $5.0\ \mathrm{g}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1}$ fungi fluid. The mucor membrane was made through vacuum filtration of $10~\mathrm{mL}$ fungi fluid and dried for 2 minutes at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
ZIF-8 and ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-O. ZIF-8 was synthesized by sonochemical methods [31]. $0.8925\ \mathrm{g}\ Z\mathrm{n(NO_{3})_{2}\bullet6H_{2}O}$ and $0.9852{\mathrm{~g~}}2{\mathrm{~}} $ -methylimidazole were dissolved in 30 mL and $10\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol, respectively [32]. The above two solutions were mixed and then placed in an ultrasonic synthesizer at $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $800~\mathrm{W}$ for 2h with or without a Mucor membrane addition to obtain ZIF-8 and ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-one-step (ZIF8@Mucor-O). The samples were alternately washed with methanol and ultrapure water three times and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
Synthesis of ZIF-8@Mucor-N. 0.8925g Zn(NO3)2·6H2O was dissolved in $40\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol and $0.9852\mathrm{g}$ 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIm) was dissolved in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol. The Mucor membrane was first soaked in the $Z_{\mathrm{{n}(N O_{3})_{2}}}$ -methanol solution for 10 minutes and then was rinsed with methanol. The treated membrane was placed in the 2-methylimidazole-methanol solution and subjected to an ultrasonic synthesizer, reacted at $20~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 800 W for 2h. The material was rinsed and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The above process was operated once to obtained ZIF-8@Mucor-1, repeated twice as ZIF8@Mucor-2, and so on.
## 2.2. Characterization
The surface morphology and structure were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM，JSM-IT300LA and Nova NanoSEM230). The surface functional groups were estimated by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR, Thermo Nicolet IS10, USA). The chemical composition of the materials was analyzed using an X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo K-Alpha 1063, USA). XRD X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) patterns were collected on a D/MAX 2500 VB+XX diffractometer by Cu-Kɑ radiation. Pore volume and pore size distribution were analyzed by Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET, SAP 2020) with $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms. Thermogravimetric analyses (TG, STA 449F3) were carried out at a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ from $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the presence of air. The zeta potential of materials was measured by the zeta potentiometer (ZetaPALS, Brookhaven). The concentration of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ was measured by Inductive Coupled Plasma Emission Spectrometer (ICP, ICP-OES 5100).
## 2.3. Adsorption experiment
$\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ was used to prepare a series of different concentrations of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ solution (50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 1000 mg/L). The pH of the solution was adjusted by using HCl and NaOH. The adsorption ability of Pb(II) was calculated by equation 2 and 3 in Support Information.
Static adsorption. The static adsorption experiments were as follows:
Batch adsorption experiments were carried in polyethylene bottles containing 20 mL Pb(II) solution and $20~\mathrm{mg}$ adsorbents. The influencing factors, including pH and temperature, were studied. The adsorption kinetics, isotherm, and thermodynamic data were obtained by controlling the contact time, initial concentration and temperature, respectively. The adsorbents were separated from the U(VI) solution owing to its macroscopic dimensions. Absorption-free experiments were performed under the same conditions to eliminate the effect of polyethylene bottle wall.
Dynamic adsorption. The dynamic adsorption conditions of ZIF-8@Mucor-3 were as follows: 40ml Pb(II) solution (100ppm) at $\mathrm{pH}5.2$ was drawn across a 0.0675g
ZIF-8@Mucor-3 membrane at the rate of $0.1\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{m}$ in a syringe controlled by an injection pump. The filtrate was collected 20 times within every 10 minutes
## 2.4. Equations
The mass fraction of ZIF-8 on ZIF-8 /Mucor was calculated as follows: $\begin{array}{r}{\boldsymbol{\upeta}=\frac{m_{1}}{m_{2}}\times100\%}\end{array}$
Where $\mathfrak{m}_{1}$ (g) represented the quality of ZIF-8 and ${\bf m}_{2}$ represented the quality of ZIF$8@$ Mucor composite (dried) in the TG curve.
The adsorption ability of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ of ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor composites was calculated by the following equation:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}=\left(\mathrm{C}_{0}-\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{e}}\right)\cdot\mathrm{V}/\mathrm{m}}\\ &{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{e}(\mathrm{ZIF}-\mathrm{8})}=\frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}-(1-\upeta)\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{e}(\mathrm{Mucor})}}{\upeta}}\end{array}
$$
Where $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}(\mathrm{mg/g})$ was the adsorption ability of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ of ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor composite at equilibrium state; $\mathrm{C_{0}(m g/L)}$ was the initial concentration of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ ; $\mathrm{C_{e}(m g/L)}$ was the equilibrium concentration of Pb(II); V was the volume (mL) of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ aqueous solution; m was the mass $\mathrm{(mg)}$ of the adsorbents used in the experiment.
The pseudo-second-order rate equation (4) was performed to test the kinetic processes of adsorption.
$$
\frac{\mathrm{~t~}}{Q_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{1}Q_{e}^{2}}+\frac{\mathrm{~t~}}{Q_{e}}
$$
Where $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{t}}(\mathrm{mg/g})$ was the adsorption ability of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ of ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3 at a specific time (t, min); $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{e}}(\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ was the adsorption ability of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ of absorbent at equilibrium state; $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ was the rate constants of pseudo-second-order equation.
Both the Langmuir (5) and Freundlich (6) models were applied to investigate the isotherm adsorption behaviors of the composites. The equations of both models were therefore specified respectively as shown below.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{e}}=\frac{C_{e}}{Q_{m}}+\frac{1}{{k_{L}}\cdot{Q_{m}}}}}\\ {{\ l g Q_{e}=\frac{l g C_{e}}{n}+l g k_{F}}}\end{array}
$$
Where $\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{m}}(\mathrm{mg/g})$ represented the maximum adsorption capacity; $\mathrm{Q_{e}(m g/g)}$ was the adsorption ability of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ composite at equilibrium state; $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{e}}(m g/L)}$ was the equilibrium concentration of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ ; ${\bf k}_{\mathrm{L}}(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mg})$ was the adsorption coefficient, and $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{F}}$ and n were the equilibrium constants.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Synthesis of the ZIF-8@Mucor composites
Mucor was inoculated in a suitable liquid medium under a rotating shock culture. The water shear force would lead to the continuous growth of hypha, which was a necessary condition conducive for improving the stability of the Mucor membrane made through vacuum filtration. The Mucor membrane underwent a stepwise reaction process. First, it reacted with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and the second reaction was with 2-MeIm to form ZIF-8@Mucor composite. With this strategy, a series of mycelial nanocomposite membranes with different loadings were synthesized. This method adopted $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ as seeds on the hypha surface and avoided the formation of unnecessary ZIF-8 in liquid, improving the utilization efficiency of raw materials.
The synthetic process was schematically shown in Fig.1. To prevent the selfaggregation of MOFs molecules, zinc ions were initially dispersed in solution and then adsorbed on the surface of the Mucor membrane by electrostatic interaction. Enrichment of Zinc ions anchored on the Mucor membrane provided a highly active interface to organic ligand (2-MeIm) under ultra-sonification filed. Especially, the coordination reaction of ZIF-8 was completed along the surface of the Mucor membrane within $10~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. The same operation was repeatedly carried out to control the thickness of the ZIF-8 layer anchored on the Mucor membrane. The samples at each stage were labeled as ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor- $1{\sim}3$ in sequence as shown in Fig.1.
The Mucor membrane was soaked with a methanol solution of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and then the membrane was placed in a ligand solution of 2-MeIm with ultra-sonification, which was repeated from 1 to 4 times to form ZIF-8@Mucor-1\~4 membranes. The zeropotential point of Mucor was 8.18 in Fig.S1. A formed ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor membrane was shown in Fig.1. The membranes still kept a good shape after ultrasonic treatment, suggesting that the composite materials had good stability. After a few minutes of drying, the mass of waterish ZIF-8@Mucor membranes was reduced by $90\%$ , facilitating transportation, and softening after rewetting.
This innovative synthesis flow allowed the load of ZIF-8 onto the Mucor surface in a controlled and step-wise manner, with the advantages of low cost, environmentally friendly, and easy to be scaled up. A three-point bending test was carried out to test the shear stress of the composites. As shown in Fig.2, the maximum loads of ZIF
8@Mucor-1\~4 membranes were 0.37, 0.91, 1.22 and 1.86 N, respectively. By calculation, the shear modulus of the ZIF-8@Mucor-1\~4 membranes were 210, 442, 864, 1054 MPa. From Fig.1, the ZIF-8@Mucor-3 composite was a round piece with a diameter of $3.4~\mathrm{cm}$ . Therefore, the ZIF-8@Mucor-3 composite with a diameter of 3.4 cm had the ability to withstand the pressure of 17.28 N. The significant change in shear modulus of ZIF-8@Mucor-2 to ZIF-8@Mucor-3 was probably because the third reaction achieved the completed coating of ZIF-8, which significantly improved the mechanical properties of the material.
## 3.2. Characterizations of the ZIF-8@Mucor
After the synthesis process described in section 3.1, the microstructure of the ZIF-8@Mucor-1\~4 was investigated with SEM, as shown in Fig. 3. The researchers observed that ZIF-8 gradually covered the Mucor surface as reaction time increased. Furthermore, after the fourth reaction, some ZIF-8 particles were re-grown on the ZIF8 layer. Combined with the results of three-point bending test, it suggested that the completed The properties of ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3 were further investigated. More SEM images of ZIF-8@Mucor-3 were shown in Fig.S2. As can be seen in Fig.S3, the wellintergrown polycrystalline ZIF-8 layer on the Mucor surface formed using $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ as the seeds after the third reaction. The load of ZIF-8 on Mucor in the one-step method mentioned in ZIF-8 and ZIF-8@Mucor-O of section 2.1 could synthesize ZIF8@Mucor composites properly; however, its solution contains large quantities of suspended ZIF-8 nanoparticles. The SEM images of ZIF-8@Mucor-O were shown in
Fig.3. Compared with ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-O, ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3 showed a more evenly distribution of ZIF-8 particles on the Mucor surface. According to the mapping image of ZIF-8@Mucor-3 in Fig.3e, it was noticed that ZIF-8 particles were evenly distributed on the Mucor.
The particle size of absorbents had a significant effect on adsorption capacity and kinetics. Generally, smaller particle sizes of the absorbent had higher adsorption capacity and adsorption kinetics due to more accessible active sites and large surface area. The ZIF-8 particle size distribution of ZIF-8@Mucor-3 was measured through Nano Measure software (Nano Measure 1.2.5), as shown in Fig.4a and Fig.S3. The mean particle diameter of ZIF-8 on ZIF-8@Mucor-3 was about 186.98 nm, which was lower than the resultant pure ZIF-8 nanoparticles and the ZIF-8 particle on ZIF8@Mucor-O in Fig.S3. The smaller the particle size, the better the mass-transfer efficiency of an adsorbent. Hence, ZIF-8@Mucor-3 composites might have higher mass-transfer efficiency than pure ZIF-8 nanoparticles and the ZIF-8@Mucor-O composites.
According to the above analysis, the load of ZIF-8 onto Mucor in a step-wise seeding of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ manner was more conducive to growing a ZIF-8 layer with smaller ZIF-8 size on Mucor surface, which might work better in adsorption of pollutant. The presence of the ZIF-8 layer on Mucor surface was verified by XRD, as shown in Fig.S4. There were some sharp peaks at $7.58^{\circ}$ , $10.62^{\circ}$ , $13.00^{\circ}$ and $18.26^{\circ}$ on XRD patterns of the ZIF-8@Mucor-3. Compared with synthetic ZIF-8, ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3 had a slight rightward deviation from pure ZIF-8, which might be caused by the chemical bond formed between ZIF-8 and the hyphae. This chemical bond exerted a force (towards blanks), resulting in a rightward deviation of the peak. This result indicated that the ZIF-8 was loaded onto the Mucor.
The XRD patterns of ZIF-8@Mucor-1\~4 were shown in Fig.4c. The characteristic peak of (011) was observed in XRD patterns of ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-1 and the characteristic peaks of (011), (122) and (222) were observed XRD patterns of ZIF-8@Mucor-2. Generally, the intensity of the ZIF-8 peak was continuously enhanced as the time of synthesis increased. The characteristic peaks of ZIF-8 also had a slight shift as the times of synthesis increased. The above phenomenon indicated that the increase of ZIF-8 on the Mucor surface affected the interaction between ZIF-8 and Mucor and the growth of the internal structure of ZIF-8 crystal. The mass fractions of ZIF-8 loaded were $3.66\%$ , $6.71\%$ , $12.60\%$ and $23.50\%$ mentioned in Table S1.
## 3.3. Thermal and texture Properties
The thermogravimetric properties of composites were investigated. Results from the analyses showed the TG curves of Mucor, ZIF-8 nanoparticles and ZIF-8@Mucor1\~4 composites in Fig.4d. All curves showed a slight decrease at the early stage when the temperature was approximately $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , attributable to the loss of residual free water. The quality of Mucor decreased a lot from $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and reached the bottom at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . ZIF8 nanoparticles also showed a sharp decrease when the temperature was higher than $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . A decelerating decline was observed in the ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor group after $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which meant increasing the ZIF-8 load could enhance thermal stability.
## 3.4. The formation mechanism of ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor
The FT-IR spectra of Mucor, ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3, and ZIF-8 were shown in Fig.4b. For ZIF-8, the peaks at 755 and $1566~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to the out-of-plane bending mode and tensile mode of $\mathrm{C=N}$ , respectively [33]. The bands in the region of 600-1500 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were mainly ascribed to the stretching and bending modes of the imidazole ring [34]. For pure Mucor, various functional groups were found in its FTIR spectra, such as -OH and -NH around $3400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ (amide I) at $1654~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , N-H and C-N (amide II) at $1542~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , C-O at $1050~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ related to phosphorylated proteins and alcohol, CH ( $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ) at 2924, and $880\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [35, 36]. For ZIF-8@Mucor-3 composites, the peaks at 2924, 1542, 1050 and $880\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ faded away, which proved chemical interaction between ZIF-8 and Mucor surface. A similar phenomenon was observed in other researches [37, 38]. The disappearance of the peaks was attributable to the fact that methyl was related to the methyl−methyl interaction [39].
If FTIR had confirmed the chemical interaction between ZIF-8 and Mucor surface in Fig.4b, XPS spectra record for different samples in the study would be used to analyze the interactions further. The survey scan XPS spectra of the ZIF-8@Mucor-3 sample in Fig.S5b-c showed the presence of Zn in addition to Mucor elements (C, N, O) in Fig.S5a, proving that the ZIF-8 covered the mucor surface and therefore assembled with Mucor successfully. However, there were no obvious differences in the binding energies of both $Z_{\mathrm{n}}2\mathrm{p}1/2$ and 2p1/2 in Fig.S6. This suggested that Zn metal centers in ZIF-8 should not be binding sites for Mucor hypha and electrostatic adsorption was responsible for the interaction of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and Mucor. Some researchers reported that the interaction of ZIF-8 with organics was multiple, such as hydrogen bonding and π-π stacking interaction [34, 39, 40]. Hence, the high-resolution C 1s and N 1s XPS spectra of Mucor, ZIF-8 and ZIF-8@Mucor-3 composites in Fig.5 were applied to analyze further. The N 1s spectrum of Mucor in Fig. 5 was resolved into three peaks centered at $399.53\mathrm{eV}$ and $401.38\mathrm{eV}$ , of which the peak at 399.53 eV was attributed to the amide groups (- $\left.\mathrm{-NH}_{2}\right.$ , -NH-, $\scriptstyle\mathrm{O=C-N}$ ) and the peak at $401.38\ \mathrm{eV}$ was associated with the protonated amine $\left(-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{3}{}^{+}\right)$ [41, 42]. Three peaks centered at 284.80 V, 286.30 eV and 288.01 eV on C 1s spectrum of Mucor were the chemical carbon bonds of C-C, C-O/C-N, $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ , respectively [38, 43]. In N 1s and C 1s spectra of ZIF8, three signals around 398.62 eV for N 1s and $284.8\mathrm{eV}$ and $285.70\mathrm{eV}$ for C 1s were found, respectively, which were assigned to N and C of imidazole groups based on comparison to analogous species [44-46]. In C 1s region, the peak at $286.30~\mathrm{eV}$ and 288. 01 eV for Mucor was shifted to $286.47\mathrm{eV}$ and $288.19\mathrm{eV}$ for ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3. A shift from 285.70 eV for ZIF-8 to $285.60\ \mathrm{eV}$ for ZIF-8@Mucor-3 was also observed. In the N 1s region, Fig. 5 showed a slight shift from $399.53\mathrm{~eV}$ to $399.26\mathrm{~eV}$ with respect to the amide groups. These changes in bonding energies might be rooted in the hydrogen bonding interactions of N and O in hypha with the hydrogen of the double carbon bond (CH group) of 2-methylimidazole. A similar interaction (CH···O hydrogen bonds) had been described in encapsulating caffeine in ZIF-8 [39] and other different examples [47, 48]. From N 1s spectrum of Mucor and ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3, it showed a significant shift from $401.38\ \mathrm{eV}$ for Mucor to 400. 41 eV for ZIF-8@Mucor-3. The conjugated $\pi$ bond between $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ and C in 2-MeIM, having strong electronegativity, was natural to react with $\mathrm{-NH}_{3}{}^{+}$ , resulting in the change of the binding energies.
## 3.5. Adsorption behavior
## 3.5.1 Static adsorption
The static adsorption experiments of $\operatorname{Pb}(\operatorname{II})$ by ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3, ZIF-8@Mucor4, and ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-O were shown in Fig.6a. The ZIF-8 maximum adsorption capacities of them were 1443.29, 1013.47 and $973.53~\mathrm{mg/g}$ (calculated according to Table 1 and Equations 2), respectively. The study again noticed that the composites with a higher load rate of ZIF-8 had lower maximum adsorption capacity. ZIF8@Mucor-3 had the highest maximum adsorption capacity than the other two composites being routed in smaller ZIF-8 particle sizes and the exposition of more active sites. Increasing the ZIF-8 loading rate was beneficial to the removal of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ , while excessive loading was not conducive to the utilization of ZIF-8. Hence, it was crucial to control the process of the load of ZIF-8 onto Mucor. Compared with the assembly of ZIF-8 directly, the synthesis method advocated in this work, assembly ZIF8 by a step-wise growth of ZIF-8, was facile to control the load of ZIF-8 onto Mucor.
## 3.5.2 Effects of pH
The initial pH of an aqueous solution was a key parameter in the adsorption process. It affected not only the surface properties of the adsorbent but also the existence form of the metals. As can be seen in Fig.6b, the adsorption capacity increased as pH increases from 2.0 to 5.0, while it decreased slightly at $\mathrm{pH}6.0$ . At low pH values, such as $\mathrm{pH}{=}2$ , there was an excess of $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ ions in the solution, which made a competition between the positively charged hydrogen ions and ${\mathrm{Pb}}({\mathrm{II}})$ ions for the available adsorption sites [49]. When the $\mathfrak{p H}$ value was greater than 5.0, lead hydroxide such as $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}{}^{-1}$ began to form, resulting in a decrease of Pb(II) adsorption on ZIF8@Mucor-3 surface [50-51].
## 3.5.3 Adsorption kinetics and isotherms
The adsorption amount of Pb(II) onto ZIF-8@Mucor-3 as a function of contact time was shown in Fig.6c. It was observed that within the initial 20 minutes, the adsorption of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ was fast. However, no significant change was observed after 120 min. The adsorption kinetics of $\mathrm{Pb}$ (II) on ZIF-8 $@$ Mucor-3 followed the pseudosecond-order model $(\mathrm{R}^{2}>0.999)$ , indicating the rate-limiting step for the adsorption of Pb (II) was chemisorption. Equilibrium isotherm studies were carried out with different initial concentrations of Pb (II) $(50{-}500~\mathrm{mg/L})$ at $\mathrm{T}{=}30\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{pH}=5$ in Fig.S7. The adsorption data were well fitted with Langmuir models $(\mathrm{R}^{2}>0.99)$ ). (Eqs.4-5 and Table 1). This capacity was much higher than previously reported in Table S2.
## 3.5.4 Dynamic adsorption
ZIF-8@Mucor-3 had good porosity as mentioned earlier in this study. The breakthrough curve of $\mathrm{Pb(II)}$ on ZIF-8@Mucor-3 was shown in Fig.6d. From the curve, the penetration concentration of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions was less than $5\%$ of the original level before
80 minutes whereas. After 160 minutes, the penetration concentration of $\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}$ ions was more than $90\%$ . This indicated that the penetration time was about 80min and the drypoint time was about 160min. The dynamic adsorption could give good guidance using these materials in industrial applications.
## 4. Conclusion
Based previously reported to the modification of MOFs, this paper had provided a good way of interface science that grew MOFs on the thallus. These composite materials were used to acquire carbon materials and other materials, applying in environment and energy, etc. In summary, ZIF-8/Mucor membranes were successfully synthesized. ZIF-8/Mucor-O was synthesized by in situ reaction, while ZIF-8/MucorN was synthesized by stage adsorption reaction. According to the comparison of adsorption capacity, ZIF-8-1,2 of the latter synthesis had a higher utilization rate of ZIF-8, due to the larger ZIF-8 surface. These materials had good adsorption capacity that could reach as high as $1443.29\mathrm{mg/g}$ Moreover, the study found out that Mucor on the ZIF-8-3 material can form a layer of ZIF-8, which greatly improved its mechanical properties. Furthermore, the synthesis method of ZIF-8/Mucor provided a critical path for interfacial compounding of other MOFs and organisms, and it could form a composite material with high loading rate and good performance.
